course: dictionary
lesson: 6192
title: Misdirection
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Misdirection means making people look at the wrong thing, so they miss what is really happening. Magicians use it all the time. «Magicians use misdirection (making you look away from the trick)» to fool you on purpose.
explain: The word can also mean sending someone the wrong way or giving wrong instructions. Look at its parts: mis- means wrong, and direction means the way to go. So misdirection is a wrong direction, whether it is for your eyes or your feet.
explain: Look for the clues in the sentences around it. «The heart of most tricks is misdirection, making you look one way while the secret happens somewhere else.» When you see a trick or an advert, ask yourself: what am I not being shown?
fact: Misdirection means making people look at or think about the wrong thing.
fact: «Misdirection» means steering someone's attention away from the secret move.
fact: Magicians use sleight of hand (quick hand tricks) and misdirection to fool the senses on purpose.
fact: Misdirection can also mean sending someone the wrong way or giving wrong instructions.
fact: Scammers and some adverts use misdirection too, so pay attention to what you are not being shown.
exercise: «Magicians use misdirection, making you look away from the trick.» What does misdirection mean here? | Showing you the secret move slowly | *Making you look at the wrong thing so you miss the secret move | Doing the same trick twice | Giving you a prize at the end of the show
exercise: «Misdirection steers your attention away from the secret move.» What does steers mean here? | Stops your attention completely, forever | *Guides or turns your attention in a different direction | Hides a coin inside your sleeve | Makes a loud noise to wake people up
exercise: Sam says, «The advert shows a smiling family but hides the price in tiny writing.» Which part is an example of misdirection? | *The smiling family pulls your eyes away from the price | The price is written in big, bold letters | Tiny writing is always easy to read | The family is smiling because it is a sunny day
test: A mystery writer uses misdirection to hide the real culprit. What does culprit mean? | The detective who solves the case | A cup of tea left on the table | *The person who did the wrong thing | The house where the story starts
test: «Sending someone the wrong way» is the second meaning of misdirection. Which example shows this? | A bus that arrives early | A sweet shop on the corner | *A sign that points the wrong way to the station | A map that shows the right road
exam: Which sentence shows misdirection in its first meaning? | A boat sails across the lake | The sun sets behind the hill | *A magician waves one hand so you miss the coin in the other | A friend tells you the time
